## 1 Introduction

The era of quantum computing already started. Several gigantic IT corporations have been devoted to develop quantum computing devices, and some of them are now providing quantum computing cloud service to the public [1, 2]. But the scale of the devices are still small (dozens of qubits), and therefore applications for them are also very limited.

Suppose that you have a quantum computing hardware and a quantum algorithm. What do you have to do to run the algorithm with the hardware? Quantum algorithm is a logical description of how to solve a given problem. It is usually based on ideal quantum computing hardware with noiseless gate and long qubit interaction. However, in reality, a quantum computer as a physical entity has a certain physical and logical limitation.

The principle of a quantum system mapping is straightforward. Initialize a quantum computer architecture and recast a quantum algorithm for the architecture. An architecture-specific description of a quantum algorithm, the main product of the system mapping, is called a **system code** in this work.

Quantum algorithm for the system mapping is provided in a **quantum assembly code (QASM)** format. There are two kinds of QASMs in terms of structure: **non-modular type** and **modular type**. A modular QASM is hierarchically structured with multiple functions called a module.

**Table 1**: The QASM size comparison over Shor's factoring algorithm. The QASMs are generated by using the compiler ScaffCC [4, 7].

\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
Input Size & Non-Modular & Modular \\
\hline
128 & 1.7 TB & 23.5 MB \\
256 & 14.2 TB & 88.1 MB \\
512 & 39.0 TB & 338.6 MB \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

We obtained a 39 TB sized non-modular QASM for Shor algorithm to factorize a 512-bit integer. Due to the lack of classical storage and memory, we could not even attempt to generate a non-modular QASM for a larger quantum algorithm.

Suppose that there is an \(n\)-qubit composite quantum operation \(U\) composed of \(K\) quantum gates and it is called as much as \(N\) times. To represent such \(N\) iterations, a non-modular QASM requires \(K\times N\) quantum instructions, but a modular QASM only requires \(K+N\) instructions by defining \(U\) as a module. Empirically, the values of \(K,N\) are not small in a non-trivial quantum algorithm.\({}^{1}\)

Footnote 1: In general, \(K\) is \(O(10^{2})\) and \(N\) is \(O(10^{4})\sim O(10^{6})\) in Shor algorithm.

## 2 Hierarchical System Mapping

### 2.1 Hierarchically Structured Qubit Layouts

Modules are arranged on a 1D array with a communication bus between them; qubits inside each module are arranged on a 2D array. Local qubits are initialized/manipulated/measured within a module; **parameter qubits** are passed between modules over a communication bus.

### 2.2 Mapping Algorithm

For a module \(M\) with \(n\) qubits, a local table is initialized:
\[\text{time}[q_i]=0,\quad\text{cycle}[q_i]=0,\quad i=1\ldots n.\]

As mapping proceeds,
\[\text{time}[q_i]\leftarrow\text{time}[q_i]+\text{wait}_t+u_t,\quad\text{cycle}[q_i]\leftarrow\text{cycle}[q_i]+\text{wait}_c+1.\]

On finishing:
\[\text{time}[M]=\max_i\text{time}[q_i],\quad\text{cycle}[M]=\max_i\text{cycle}[q_i].\]

Module-call procedure (seven steps): 1. (forward) move qubits to the bus, 2. (forward) move to the target module, 3. (forward) move to the parameter qubit cells, 4. module operations, 5. (backward) move qubits to the bus, 6. (backward) move to the original module, 7. (backward) move to the original qubit positions.

Once mapped, module performance is cached; subsequent calls reuse the cached result. This is the algorithmic origin of the mapping-time speedup.

## 3 Discussion

The mapping for Shor-512 with a non-modular QASM requires ~\(1500\) days, but the proposed mapping can be done within ~\(1\) hour. Implementation was in Python on a 3.5 GHz CPU / 128 GB RAM machine.

**Table 2**: Quantum resource comparison, Shor algorithm. Hierarchical qubit column shows "computing qubits (bus qubits)".

\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\hline
Algorithm & Non-Modular Qubits & Non-Modular SWAPs & Hierarchical Qubits & Hierarchical SWAPs \\
\hline
Shor-4  & 46  & \(4.08\times 10^{4}\) & 115 (374)   & \(1.23\times 10^{6}\) \\
Shor-8  & 90  & \(4.71\times 10^{5}\) & 227 (1{,}122) & \(1.02\times 10^{7}\) \\
Shor-16 & 178 & \(5.82\times 10^{6}\) & 448 (3{,}055) & \(7.66\times 10^{7}\) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

**Trade-off:** hierarchical uses ~\(2.5\times\) more computing qubits + arbitrary bus qubits, and more raw SWAPs. However only ~10% of gates are SWAPs and ~99% of SWAPs occur during qubit passing (parallelizable); only 1/2 to 1/3 actually contribute to circuit depth. SWAP-ratio hierarchical/non-modular shrinks with input size: \(30.3\times \to 21.8\times \to 13.16\times\).

Within-module CNOT-SWAP overhead (proposed vs non-modular): \(0.04\) vs \(1.00\) (Shor-4); \(0.02\) vs \(1.42\) (Shor-8); \(0.02\) vs \(2.18\) (Shor-16). For proposed only: \(0.009\) (Shor-128), \(0.007\) (Shor-256), \(0.005\) (Shor-512).

Qubit-placement optimization via linear programming [8] gives \(<0.01\%\) circuit-depth reduction for Shor (negligible) but \(1{-}6\%\) for BWT algorithm (e.g. \(2.42\times 10^{5}\to 2.26\times 10^{5}\) for BWT-10).

## 4 Conclusion

Future work: more realistic architecture [12]; improved mapping algorithm; account for QEC / non-transversal-gate variable latencies; communication-bus congestion for highly parallel applications [13]; improve spatial efficiency (most qubits idle waiting during most of the execution).

